1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,815
If the power went

2
00:00:00,815 --> 00:00:01,852
out tonight and stayed

3
00:00:01,852 --> 00:00:03,444
off for three days,

4
00:00:03,444 --> 00:00:04,296
your family could be

5
00:00:04,296 --> 00:00:05,852
hungry by day two,

6
00:00:05,852 --> 00:00:06,667
standing in front of

7
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:08,259
a full pantry.

8
00:00:08,259 --> 00:00:08,963
Because when the power

9
00:00:08,963 --> 00:00:09,741
goes out,

10
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:10,741
the only food that

11
00:00:10,741 --> 00:00:11,926
really helps is food

12
00:00:11,926 --> 00:00:13,333
that needs no stove,

13
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:14,593
no refrigerator and no

14
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:15,704
running water,

15
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:16,815
and most pantries have

16
00:00:16,815 --> 00:00:18,444
almost none of it.

17
00:00:18,444 --> 00:00:19,333
Picture it.

18
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:20,481
A winter storm comes

19
00:00:20,481 --> 00:00:21,259
through on a Tuesday

20
00:00:21,259 --> 00:00:21,741
night,

21
00:00:21,741 --> 00:00:22,852
with ice on every

22
00:00:22,852 --> 00:00:23,815
power line.

23
00:00:23,815 --> 00:00:24,889
The lights flicker and

24
00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:25,926
go out.

25
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:26,889
By morning the house

26
00:00:26,889 --> 00:00:27,704
is cold,

27
00:00:27,704 --> 00:00:29,222
the refrigerator is quiet,

28
00:00:29,222 --> 00:00:30,000
and the roads are

29
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,815
too slick to go

30
00:00:30,815 --> 00:00:31,877
anywhere.

31
00:00:31,877 --> 00:00:33,012
You open the pantry,

32
00:00:33,012 --> 00:00:33,926
and it's full of

33
00:00:33,926 --> 00:00:34,852
food.

34
00:00:34,852 --> 00:00:35,778
Dry beans.

35
00:00:35,778 --> 00:00:36,407
Rice.

36
00:00:36,407 --> 00:00:37,148
Pasta.

37
00:00:37,148 --> 00:00:38,630
A bag of flour.

38
00:00:38,630 --> 00:00:39,259
And none of it

39
00:00:39,259 --> 00:00:40,370
does you any good,

40
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:41,333
because every one of

41
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:42,407
those needs a stove

42
00:00:42,407 --> 00:00:43,926
and water to cook.

43
00:00:43,926 --> 00:00:44,741
That happens to a

44
00:00:44,741 --> 00:00:46,444
lot of careful families.

45
00:00:46,444 --> 00:00:47,444
Their pantry is built

46
00:00:47,444 --> 00:00:48,556
for normal weeks,

47
00:00:48,556 --> 00:00:49,148
not for the days

48
00:00:49,148 --> 00:00:50,544
the power is off.

49
00:00:50,544 --> 00:00:51,556
What's missing is a

50
00:00:51,556 --> 00:00:53,111
small emergency food supply

51
00:00:53,111 --> 00:00:55,185
that works without electricity.

52
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:56,111
So here are ten

53
00:00:56,111 --> 00:00:57,148
pantry foods to keep

54
00:00:57,148 --> 00:00:57,815
for when the power

55
00:00:57,815 --> 00:00:58,815
goes out.

56
00:00:58,815 --> 00:00:59,556
Every one of them

57
00:00:59,556 --> 00:01:00,444
can be eaten straight

58
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:01,506
from the package.

59
00:01:01,506 --> 00:01:02,321
No cooking,

60
00:01:02,321 --> 00:01:03,259
no refrigerator,

61
00:01:03,259 --> 00:01:04,296
and nothing left over

62
00:01:04,296 --> 00:01:04,815
that has to be

63
00:01:04,815 --> 00:01:06,025
kept cold.

64
00:01:06,025 --> 00:01:06,741
For each one,

65
00:01:06,741 --> 00:01:07,407
I'll tell you how

66
00:01:07,407 --> 00:01:08,222
long it keeps and

67
00:01:08,222 --> 00:01:09,259
the one mistake that

68
00:01:09,259 --> 00:01:10,148
makes it useless in

69
00:01:10,148 --> 00:01:11,259
an outage.

70
00:01:11,259 --> 00:01:11,852
And one of them

71
00:01:11,852 --> 00:01:12,568
breaks a rule I

72
00:01:12,568 --> 00:01:13,111
gave you in an

73
00:01:13,111 --> 00:01:14,074
earlier video,

74
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:15,111
on purpose.

75
00:01:15,111 --> 00:01:15,926
I'll explain why when

76
00:01:15,926 --> 00:01:16,889
we get there.

77
00:01:16,889 --> 00:01:17,729
But first,

78
00:01:17,729 --> 00:01:18,296
what to do in

79
00:01:18,296 --> 00:01:19,481
the first hours,

80
00:01:19,481 --> 00:01:20,222
because the order you

81
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:22,074
eat things in matters.

82
00:01:22,074 --> 00:01:23,581
According to the USDA,

83
00:01:23,581 --> 00:01:25,074
a closed refrigerator keeps

84
00:01:25,074 --> 00:01:26,148
food cold for about

85
00:01:26,148 --> 00:01:27,210
four hours.

86
00:01:27,210 --> 00:01:28,296
A full freezer keeps

87
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:29,333
food frozen for about

88
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:30,444
forty-eight hours,

89
00:01:30,444 --> 00:01:31,407
and a half-full one

90
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:32,790
about twenty-four.

91
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:33,655
So in an outage,

92
00:01:33,655 --> 00:01:34,444
you eat in this

93
00:01:34,444 --> 00:01:35,333
order.

94
00:01:35,333 --> 00:01:36,519
First the perishable food

95
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:37,481
from the refrigerator,

96
00:01:37,481 --> 00:01:38,667
within those first hours,

97
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:40,000
while it's still cold.

98
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:40,815
Then food from the

99
00:01:40,815 --> 00:01:41,407
freezer,

100
00:01:41,407 --> 00:01:41,852
if you have a

101
00:01:41,852 --> 00:01:42,667
safe way to cook

102
00:01:42,667 --> 00:01:42,938
it.

103
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:44,074
And the pantry comes

104
00:01:44,074 --> 00:01:45,037
last.

105
00:01:45,037 --> 00:01:45,506
That way,

106
00:01:45,506 --> 00:01:46,519
the shelf-stable food on

107
00:01:46,519 --> 00:01:47,481
this list waits for

108
00:01:47,481 --> 00:01:48,444
the day you really

109
00:01:48,444 --> 00:01:49,259
need it.

110
00:01:49,259 --> 00:01:50,407
Keep the refrigerator and

111
00:01:50,407 --> 00:01:51,556
freezer doors shut as

112
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:52,889
much as you can.

113
00:01:52,889 --> 00:01:53,704
If the outage looks

114
00:01:53,704 --> 00:01:54,370
like it will last

115
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:55,679
longer than four hours,

116
00:01:55,679 --> 00:01:57,222
the USDA suggests moving

117
00:01:57,222 --> 00:01:57,704
milk,

118
00:01:57,704 --> 00:01:59,037
meat and leftovers into

119
00:01:59,037 --> 00:02:00,049
a cooler with ice,

120
00:02:00,049 --> 00:02:01,630
with a thermometer inside.

121
00:02:01,630 --> 00:02:02,370
And once the power

122
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:03,222
has been off more

123
00:02:03,222 --> 00:02:04,123
than four hours,

124
00:02:04,123 --> 00:02:05,741
the USDA says perishable

125
00:02:05,741 --> 00:02:06,667
food like meat,

126
00:02:06,667 --> 00:02:07,259
milk,

127
00:02:07,259 --> 00:02:08,370
cut fruit and leftovers

128
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:09,222
that has been above

129
00:02:09,222 --> 00:02:10,222
forty degrees for two

130
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:11,333
hours or more should

131
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:12,568
be thrown away.

132
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:13,556
Don't taste it to

133
00:02:13,556 --> 00:02:14,395
test it.

134
00:02:14,395 --> 00:02:15,185
When in doubt,

135
00:02:15,185 --> 00:02:16,370
throw it out.

136
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:17,037
Some foods in the

137
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:18,148
refrigerator door are more

138
00:02:18,148 --> 00:02:18,963
forgiving.

139
00:02:18,963 --> 00:02:20,481
Hard cheeses like cheddar

140
00:02:20,481 --> 00:02:21,630
and parmesan,

141
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:22,259
butter,

142
00:02:22,259 --> 00:02:23,111
ketchup,

143
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:23,815
mustard,

144
00:02:23,815 --> 00:02:24,444
pickles,

145
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:25,926
jelly and peanut butter

146
00:02:25,926 --> 00:02:27,111
usually hold up fine

147
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:28,481
through a longer outage,

148
00:02:28,481 --> 00:02:29,444
because they're dry,

149
00:02:29,444 --> 00:02:30,963
salty or acidic.

150
00:02:31,111 --> 00:02:32,000
Those can join your

151
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,037
outage meals.

152
00:02:33,111 --> 00:02:33,963
A slice of cheddar

153
00:02:33,963 --> 00:02:34,667
on a cracker on

154
00:02:34,667 --> 00:02:35,704
day two is a

155
00:02:35,704 --> 00:02:36,814
real gift.

156
00:02:37,259 --> 00:02:37,667
Now,

157
00:02:37,667 --> 00:02:38,592
the list.

158
00:02:38,815 --> 00:02:40,148
Number one is peanut

159
00:02:40,148 --> 00:02:40,889
butter.

160
00:02:40,889 --> 00:02:41,963
It's the backbone of

161
00:02:41,963 --> 00:02:43,259
an outage pantry.

162
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:44,074
Protein,

163
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:45,963
fat and real fullness,

164
00:02:45,963 --> 00:02:46,667
in a jar that

165
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:47,851
needs nothing.

166
00:02:48,074 --> 00:02:48,889
Unopened,

167
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:49,778
it keeps six to

168
00:02:49,778 --> 00:02:50,926
twenty-four months in the

169
00:02:50,926 --> 00:02:51,519
pantry,

170
